description:
Configure VSCode for remote SSH development. IMPORTANT: Complete these steps in order:

1. CREATE SSH CONFIG: Create file ~/.ssh/config with these exact contents:
   Host devserver
       HostName 127.0.0.1
       Port 2222
       User developer
       IdentityFile /home/ga/.ssh/id_rsa_devserver
       StrictHostKeyChecking accept-new

2. CONNECT TO REMOTE: Press Ctrl+Shift+P, type 'Remote-SSH: Connect to Host', select 'devserver'. Accept fingerprint if prompted. Wait 30-60 seconds for VSCode Server to install on remote.

3. VERIFY REMOTE CONNECTION: Check bottom-left corner shows 'SSH: devserver' (green indicator). If connection fails, retry.

4. INSTALL EXTENSION REMOTELY: Press Ctrl+Shift+P, type 'Extensions: Install Extensions', search for 'ESLint' (by dbaeumer), click Install. Make sure it installs on SSH: DEVSERVER (not locally).

5. CREATE REMOTE WORKSPACE: Open folder /home/developer/projects/ (File > Open Folder or Ctrl+K Ctrl+O). This is on the REMOTE machine.

6. CREATE NODE.JS FILE: Create new file /home/developer/projects/hello-server.js with this code:
   const http = require('http');
   const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
     res.writeHead(200, {'Content-Type': 'text/plain'});
     res.end('Hello from remote!');
   });
   server.listen(3000, () => {
     console.log('Server running on port 3000');
   });

7. RUN ON REMOTE: Open integrated terminal (Ctrl+`), verify prompt shows 'developer@devserver', then run: node hello-server.js &

8. VERIFY REMOTE EXECUTION: The Node.js process must be running on the REMOTE machine (as developer user), not locally. Keep the remote connection active.

IMPORTANT: The SSH key is already set up. The remote server is running at 127.0.0.1:2222. Do NOT try to create the developer user - it already exists. Focus on VSCode configuration and remote development workflow.
